title: test_tts_interrupt subprocess/pidfd tests flake under full-suite CPU load
updated_at: "2026-08-22T21:57:04Z"
---
Three different tests in tests/test_tts_interrupt.py failed across four full-suite runs on 2026-08-23, each passing alone and with its whole file (268 passed, 14 skipped):

- test_portable_result_eof_retains_authority_through_supervisor_atexit
- test_direct_children_open_publishes_before_delivered_sigint
- test_outer_pidfd_is_owned_before_delivered_sigint

They spawn real subprocesses and wait on short budgets (e.g. proc.communicate(timeout=2.0) at tests/test_tts_interrupt.py:2658) plus pidfd ownership races, so they lose under concurrent CPU load.

Not caused by any src change: reproduced on master both before and after the arch/compact-once merge, and the same candidate-1 code produced a fully green run (6042 passed, 102 skipped) in its own worktree.

Fix direction: make the waits condition-based rather than fixed short timeouts, or widen the budgets when the machine is loaded. Do not paper over it with retries.

Distinct from a second, environmental failure mode seen the same day: a fresh git worktree venv has only runtime deps, so `uv run pytest` falls back to a system interpreter and the `[sys.executable, "-m", "hark.tts_worker"]` spawn at src/hark/tts_worker.py:1159 cannot import hark. `uv sync --extra dev` in the worktree fixes that one.


## 2026-08-23 follow-up: two distinct mechanisms, not one

Investigation while landing the architecture-review candidates separated the variance into two independent causes. Do not treat them as one bug.

**(a) 14 hasattr-gated pidfd tests flip between run and skip.** `os.pidfd_open` does NOT exist in this uv-managed CPython 3.13.11 (`python -c "import os; print(hasattr(os,'pidfd_open'))"` -> False), so `@pytest.mark.skipif(not hasattr(os, "pidfd_open"))` at tests/test_tts_interrupt.py:1523,1566 should always skip. `skipif` is evaluated per item at SETUP, and tests/test_ask_interrupt.py:1315 installs `pidfd_open` onto `capture_mod.os` (the shared `os` module) with `raising=False`. Whether that attribute is present when a later item's setup runs decides run-vs-skip. Evidence: the same tree gave `6053 passed / 88 skipped` and `6040 passed / 102 skipped` on consecutive full runs — a delta of exactly 14. NOTE: running `test_ask_interrupt.py` then `test_tts_interrupt.py` in one process does NOT reproduce it (324 passed, 14 skipped), so the leak trigger is some other file or a fixture, not that simple ordering. Still to be pinned.

**(b) Load-sensitive subprocess budgets.** Separate from (a). `test_outer_pidfd_is_owned_before_delivered_sigint` is gated on `sys.platform` only (line 1428), always runs, passes standalone in 0.15s, and failed only in a full suite under concurrent CPU load. Same family: `test_tts_play_queue.py::test_claim_lock_timeout_reports_owner_and_queue` failed once with `subprocess.TimeoutExpired ... after 2.0 seconds` and passes standalone (30 passed).

**(c) Not a mechanism, but a trap that looks like one.** A fresh git worktree venv holds only runtime deps, so `uv run pytest` falls back to a system interpreter and the `[sys.executable, "-m", "hark.tts_worker"]` spawn at src/hark/tts_worker.py:1159 cannot import hark. Fix with `uv sync --extra dev` in the worktree. Also note a fresh worktree venv lacks the `wake` / `smart-turn` extras that the main checkout has, which shifts skip counts by ~28 — so never compare skip totals across checkouts.


## 2026-08-23: third instance, signal-handling timing

`tests/test_ask_interrupt.py::test_capture_sigint_aborts_stream_and_restores_handlers` fails roughly 1 in 12 runs when run as a single item, measured 12x on two different trees: master (1/12) and the arch/audio-seams refactor branch (1/12). Equal rates, so it is NOT caused by the frame-source refactor — it is another pre-existing timing flake, this one in SIGINT delivery / handler restoration rather than a subprocess budget.

Method note for whoever picks this up: single runs mislead here. The first probe showed "fails on the branch, passes on master" and reversed on re-run; only a repeat count separated them. Measure a rate, do not compare one run to one run.